17:["$","main",null,{"id":"top","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface/40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10 sm:px-8","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"/operating-system","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-mute transition hover:text-accent","children":"← All chapters"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent/12 px-2.5 py-0.5","children":"Chapter 8"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-mute","children":["· ",19," min read · illustrated"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-hand text-5xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-6xl","children":"The Process & the PCB"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-lg text-mute","children":"What a running program actually is to the kernel, and the record it keeps to manage one"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-3","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"The last chapter ended on a definition: a process is a program brought to life, a private address space, a set of CPU registers, some open files, and an entry in the kernel’s bookkeeping. This chapter takes that definition apart and rebuilds it from the inside. We want to know precisely what the kernel stores about each running program, how it flips one on and off the CPU, and how new processes are born and eventually die."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"Everything here rests on one data structure: the Process Control Block, the per-process record the kernel keeps for every process alive on the machine. Once you can picture the PCB, the PID, the saved registers, the memory maps, the open-file table, the state, the rest of the process model falls out of it almost mechanically. Context switching is saving one PCB and loading another. Scheduling is choosing which PCB runs next. A zombie is a PCB that has not been cleaned up yet."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"Then we get concrete with the four system calls that define the Unix process model, fork, exec, wait, and exit, and write real C that creates children, replaces process images, and reaps the dead. This is the exact machinery a shell uses every time you type a command, that a web server uses to handle a request, and that Postgres uses to isolate every client connection. Understanding it is understanding how software actually runs."}]]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 py-12 sm:px-8","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"sticky top-8 hidden h-fit w-56 shrink-0 lg:block","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-hand text-xl font-bold text-ink","children":"On this page"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-program-vs-process",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-program-vs-process","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"What a process really is"}]]}]}],["$","li","02-address-space-recap",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#02-address-space-recap","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"02"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"What the process owns in memory"}]]}]}],["$","li","03-the-pcb",{"children":"$L21"}],"$L22","$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26","$L27"]}]]}],"$L28"]}],"$L29"]}]
