40:["$","span",null,{"children":"MySQL (InnoDB) uses one thread per connection inside a single process, lighter weight, shared caches, but a bug can affect the whole server."}]
41:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Depth ahead",": "]}],"Process-per-connection vs thread-per-connection is a real architectural fork in the road, and both choices trace straight back to this chapter. Next we open the process up and study the threads inside it, and the synchronization the shared address space demands."]}]]}]
42:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L34",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch08%2F09-process-vs-thread.jpg?v=ae1ede90aa3c72164e2da0809c6889b4","alt":"Hand-drawn A4 diagram: LEFT \"process model\", three separate address-space boxes each with its own text/heap/stack, labelled \"isolated, one PCB each\"; RIGHT \"thread model\", one address space box containing three stacks and program counters sharing the same heap and code, labelled \"shared memory\"; a footer names Postgres (process-per-connection) vs MySQL (thread-per-connection)."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
